.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt DMABUF 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dmabuf
.Nd native buffer sharing between devices and processes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dmabuf_base_load="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
In the kernel sources:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <dev/dmabuf/dmabuf.h>
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
core lets a driver that owns memory (an exporter) hand it to another driver or to a
process as a file descriptor, without a copy.
The descriptor is a file of its own type; it can be passed over a
.Xr unix 4
socket, mapped with
.Xr mmap 2 ,
and given to another driver, which turns it back into the buffer.
.Pp
The module is called
.Sy dmabuf_base ,
not
.Sy dmabuf :
the drm-kmod package ships a different module named
.Sy dmabuf
(the Linux dma-buf emulation of the GPU stack) with another ABI.
With the GPU stack, drm-kmod's module must be the one patched to import native descriptors; it
depends on
.Sy dmabuf_base
and is loaded after it.
.Ss Userland interface
A descriptor is obtained from a driver, for example with
.Dv VIDIOC_EXPBUF
on a
.Xr media 4
device.
The following operations are supported on it:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Xr mmap 2
Maps the buffer, if the exporter supports it.
.It Xr lseek 2
.Ql lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_END)
returns the size of the buffer; only
.Dv SEEK_SET
and
.Dv SEEK_END
are meaningful.
.It Xr fstat 2
Reports the buffer.
.It Xr ioctl 2 Dv DMA_BUF_IOCTL_SYNC
.Pq Li _IOW('b', 0, uint64_t) .
Brackets CPU access to the buffer.
The argument is
.Dv DMA_BUF_SYNC_START
(0) or
.Dv DMA_BUF_SYNC_END
(4), ORed with
.Dv DMA_BUF_SYNC_READ
(1) and/or
.Dv DMA_BUF_SYNC_WRITE
(2); at least one of read and write is required, and any other bit gives
.Er EINVAL .
The exporters' memory is uncached and coherent, so the call does no cache maintenance and only
validates its argument.
The structure and number are those of Linux's
.In linux/dma-buf.h ,
shipped in
.Pa sys/dev/media/uapi/linux/dma-buf.h .
.El
.Pp
Other ioctls fail with
.Er ENOTTY .
.Ss Kernel interface
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fn dmabuf_alloc ops size priv
Creates a buffer with one reference held by the caller.
.Fa ops
supplies
.Fn mmap ,
.Fn vmap ,
.Fn sglist
and
.Fn release .
.It Fn dmabuf_hold buf
.It Fn dmabuf_put buf
Take and drop a reference.
The last drop calls the exporter's
.Fn release .
.It Fn dmabuf_to_fd buf oflags td fdp
Creates a descriptor in the table of
.Fa td ;
the file takes its own reference.
.It Fn dmabuf_from_fd fd td bufp
Returns the buffer behind a descriptor, referenced for the caller
.Pq Er EBADF No or Er EINVAL No if it is not one .
.It Fn dmabuf_vmap buf
The kernel address of the whole buffer, or NULL.
.It Fn dmabuf_sglist buf sgp
The physical segments of the buffer as a new
.Xr sglist 9 .
.El
.Pp
The module cannot be unloaded while any buffer exists.
A buffer outlives the queue, the device and the driver that exported it as long as a descriptor
or a mapping refers to it.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES AND TUNABLES
None.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr media 4 ,
.Xr cedrus 4 ,
.Xr v4l2_stateless 7
.Sh HISTORY
The native
.Nm
core appeared together with the
.Xr media 4
core in the freebsd-media project, 2026.
